{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"preprocessor-macros","title":"Preprocessor & Macros","group":"Language Basics","order":12,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Before compilation, the preprocessor rewrites the source text: it pastes headers, expands macros and keeps or drops code with <code>#if</code>. It knows nothing about types or scopes.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Directives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Directive</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>#include &lt;x&gt;</code> / <code>#include \"x\"</code></td><td>Paste a header (system paths / project paths first)</td></tr><tr><td><code>#define NAME value</code></td><td>Object-like macro: textual replacement</td></tr><tr><td><code>#define F(a, b) (…)</code></td><td>Function-like macro</td></tr><tr><td><code>#undef NAME</code></td><td>Remove a macro</td></tr><tr><td><code>#if</code> / <code>#ifdef</code> / <code>#ifndef</code> / <code>#elif</code> / <code>#else</code> / <code>#endif</code></td><td>Conditional compilation</td></tr><tr><td><code>#pragma once</code></td><td>Include guard (non-standard but universal)</td></tr><tr><td><code>#error msg</code></td><td>Stop compilation with a message</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Macros and their traps</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\n#define SQUARE_BAD(x) x * x\n#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))\n#define MAX(a, b) ((a) &gt; (b) ? (a) : (b))\n\nconstexpr int square(int x) { return x * x; }       // the C++ way: typed, scoped, evaluated once\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; SQUARE_BAD(2 + 3) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; SQUARE(2 + 3) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; square(2 + 3) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // 2 + 3 * 2 + 3\n    int i = 5;\n    int m = MAX(i++, 0);                             // i++ is evaluated TWICE\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; m &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>11 25 25\n6 7</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Macros ignore scope and types, evaluate arguments multiple times and are invisible to the debugger. Use <code>constexpr</code> variables, <code>inline</code>/<code>constexpr</code> functions, templates and <code>enum class</code> instead.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>Where macros are still useful</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\n#define STRINGIFY(x) #x                         // # turns an argument into a string literal\n#define CONCAT(a, b) a##b                       // ## pastes tokens\n#define LOG_VAR(v) std::cout &lt;&lt; #v &lt;&lt; \" = \" &lt;&lt; (v) &lt;&lt; '\\n'\n\n#ifndef NDEBUG\n#define DEBUG_ONLY(code) code\n#else\n#define DEBUG_ONLY(code)\n#endif\n\nint main() {\n    int CONCAT(user, Count) = 3;                 // declares userCount\n    LOG_VAR(userCount * 2);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; STRINGIFY(hello world) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; __LINE__ &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (__cplusplus &gt;= 202002L) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // predefined macros\n    DEBUG_ONLY(std::cout &lt;&lt; \"debug build\\n\";)\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>userCount * 2 = 6\nhello world\n17 1\ndebug build</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Predefined macro</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>__FILE__</code>, <code>__LINE__</code></td><td>Current file name and line (C++20: prefer <code>std::source_location</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>__cplusplus</code></td><td>Standard version, e.g. <code>202002L</code> for C++20</td></tr><tr><td><code>NDEBUG</code></td><td>Defined in release builds (<code>-DNDEBUG</code>) — disables <code>assert</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>_WIN32</code>, <code>__linux__</code>, <code>__APPLE__</code></td><td>Target platform</td></tr><tr><td><code>__GNUC__</code>, <code>__clang__</code>, <code>_MSC_VER</code></td><td>Compiler</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-check\"></i><code>assert</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cassert&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint divide(int a, int b) {\n    assert(b != 0 &amp;&amp; \"divisor must be non-zero\");   // checked only when NDEBUG is not defined\n    return a / b;\n}\nint main() { std::cout &lt;&lt; divide(10, 2) &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why prefer constexpr over #define for constants?</summary><div><p>A <code>constexpr</code> constant has a type (so the compiler checks its uses), respects namespaces and scopes, shows up in the debugger, and can't be silently redefined elsewhere. A <code>#define</code> is blind text replacement that can clash with any identifier of the same name in any header included later.</p></div></details></section>"}